        <Literal xml:lang="en">Amperometry can be distinguished from voltammetry by the parameter being controlled (electrode potential E) and the parameter being measured (electrode current I which is usually a function of time – see chronoamperometry).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_3fe63496_6d87_427a_b2aa_4555bc31ee1a</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">In a non-stirred solution, a diffusion-limited current is usually measured, which is propor-tional to the concentration of an electroactive analyte.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_3fe63496_6d87_427a_b2aa_4555bc31ee1a</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The current is usually faradaic and the applied potential is usually constant.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_3fe63496_6d87_427a_b2aa_4555bc31ee1a</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The integral of current with time is the electric charge, which may be related to the amount of substance reacted by Faraday’s laws of electrolysis.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
