        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b3e56313_417c_42b7_a934_0018ce2dffc2</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">voltammetry in which small potential pulses (constant height 10 to 100 mV, constant width 10 to 100 ms) are superimposed onto a linearly varying potential or onto a staircase potential ramp. The current is sampled just before the onset of the pulse (e.g. 10 to 20 ms) and for the same sampling time just before the end of the pulse. The difference between the two sampled currents is plotted versus the potential applied before the pulse. Thus, a differential pulse voltammogram is peak-shaped</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b3e56313_417c_42b7_a934_0018ce2dffc2</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Differential_pulse_voltammetry</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_fe015383_afb3_44a6_ae86_043628697aa2"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b3e56313_417c_42b7_a934_0018ce2dffc2</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b3e56313_417c_42b7_a934_0018ce2dffc2</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Differential pulse polarography is differential pulse voltammetry in which a dropping mercury electrode is used as the working electrode. A pulse is applied before the mechani- cally enforced end of the drop and the current is sampled twice: just before the onset of the pulse and just before its end. The pulse width is usually 10 to 20 % of the drop life. The drop dislodgement is synchronized with current sampling, which is carried out as in DPV.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b3e56313_417c_42b7_a934_0018ce2dffc2</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The ratio of faradaic current to charging current is enhanced and the negative influence of charging current is partially eliminated in the same way as in normal pulse voltammetry (NPV). Moreover, subtraction of the charging current sampled before the application of the pulse further decreases its negative influence. Due to the more enhanced signal (faradaic current) to noise (charging current) ratio, the limit of detection is lower than with NPV.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b3e56313_417c_42b7_a934_0018ce2dffc2</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The sensitivity of DPV depends on the reversibility of the electrode reaction of the analyte.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b3e56313_417c_42b7_a934_0018ce2dffc2</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">DPV</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b3e56313_417c_42b7_a934_0018ce2dffc2</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">DifferentialPulseVoltammetry</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b3eb8c65_5644_45e3_9e17_0be6277c7962</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">NMCElectrode</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b3eb8c65_5644_45e3_9e17_0be6277c7962</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">LithiumNickelManganeseCobaltOxideElectrode</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b442217f_02f6_4991_aadf_22d81369223c</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">a coin case with a nominal diameter of 11.6 mm and a height of 3.1 mm</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b442217f_02f6_4991_aadf_22d81369223c</IRI>
        <Literal>R54</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b442217f_02f6_4991_aadf_22d81369223c</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">R1131</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b5039bc8_f595_4797_908a_7e86131fbde4</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">two-step electrochemical measurement in which 1) material is accumulated at an electrode and 2) the material is removed by chemical reaction or electrochemically at constant current with measurement of electrode potential. The time between changes in potential in step 2 is related to the concentration of analyte in the solution</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_fe015383_afb3_44a6_ae86_043628697aa2"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b5039bc8_f595_4797_908a_7e86131fbde4</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b5039bc8_f595_4797_908a_7e86131fbde4</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Historically for the analysis of metal ions, mercury ions were added to the test solution to form a mercury amalgam when reduced. Alternatively, an HMDE or MFE was used and the oxidizing agent added after amalgam formation. However, the toxicity of mercury and its compounds have all but precluded the present-day use of mercury.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b5039bc8_f595_4797_908a_7e86131fbde4</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The accumulation is similar to that used in stripping voltammetry.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b5039bc8_f595_4797_908a_7e86131fbde4</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The stripping potentiogram shows staircase curves of potential as a function of time. Fre- quently, the first derivative is displayed (dE/dt = f(t)), as this produces peak-shaped signals. The time between transitions (peaks) is proportional to the concentration of analyte in the test solution.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b5039bc8_f595_4797_908a_7e86131fbde4</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">PSA</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b5039bc8_f595_4797_908a_7e86131fbde4</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">PotentiometricStrippingAnalysis</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.org/dc/terms/source"/>
            <Literal>A. J. Bard, G. Inzelt, and F. Scholz, Eds., Electrochemical Dictionary, 2nd Edition. Berlin: Springer-Verlag, 2012. DOI: http://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-29551-5</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b525a629_a679_464f_bc5b_b49d2fc82686</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">an electrochemical cell for conductivity measurements</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b525a629_a679_464f_bc5b_b49d2fc82686</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Formed, in theory, by two 1 cm2 reversible electrodes spaced 1 cm apart, providing a uniform distribution of electrical field. In practice, however, a number
of other configurations are used.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b525a629_a679_464f_bc5b_b49d2fc82686</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">ConductivityCell</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b56a95e6_eea5_4a58_99aa_045fd28e75d3</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">a coin case with a nominal diameter of 5.8 mm and a height of 2.7 mm</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b56a95e6_eea5_4a58_99aa_045fd28e75d3</IRI>
        <Literal>R64</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b56a95e6_eea5_4a58_99aa_045fd28e75d3</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">R527</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b5e91259_cd97_4ed6_9ab2_4b18ef68a35a</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">LithiumVanadiumOxideElectrode</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b5ea31c9_bb10_4276_980e_c3eceb1efc82</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A constituent in an electrochemical system.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b5ea31c9_bb10_4276_980e_c3eceb1efc82</IRI>
        <Literal>a constituent is essential to the functioning of the system and cannot be replaced by a comparable thing without altering the behaviour of the system</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b5ea31c9_bb10_4276_980e_c3eceb1efc82</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">ElectrochemicalConstituent</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_50c298c2_55a2_4068_b3ac_4e948c33181f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b6319c74_d2ce_48c0_a75a_63156776b302</IRI>
        <Literal>https://www.electropedia.org/iev/iev.nsf/display?openform&amp;ievref=482-02-27</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_6dd685dd_1895_46e4_b227_be9f7d643c25"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b6319c74_d2ce_48c0_a75a_63156776b302</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://dbpedia.org/page/Anode</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.org/dc/terms/source"/>
            <Literal>International Electrotechnical Commission (IEC), IEC 60050 - International Electrotechnical Vocabulary, retrieved from: https://www.electropedia.org</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b6319c74_d2ce_48c0_a75a_63156776b302</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">by convention, cell electrode at which an oxidation reaction occurs</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b6319c74_d2ce_48c0_a75a_63156776b302</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anode</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_fe015383_afb3_44a6_ae86_043628697aa2"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b6319c74_d2ce_48c0_a75a_63156776b302</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00370</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.org/dc/terms/source"/>
            <Literal>J. M. Pingarrón et al., Terminology of electrochemical methods of analysis (IUPAC Recommendations 2019), Pure and Applied Chemistry, 4, 92, 2020, 641-694. https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b6319c74_d2ce_48c0_a75a_63156776b302</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">at an anode, electrons are produced in a galvanic cell or extracted in an electrolytic cell</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.org/dc/terms/source"/>
            <Literal>J. M. Pingarrón et al., Terminology of electrochemical methods of analysis (IUPAC Recommendations 2019), Pure and Applied Chemistry, 4, 92, 2020, 641-694. https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b6319c74_d2ce_48c0_a75a_63156776b302</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">electrode of an electrochemical cell through which net electric current flows and at which the predominating electrochemical reaction is an oxidation</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.org/dc/terms/source"/>
            <Literal>International Electrotechnical Commission (IEC), IEC 60050 - International Electrotechnical Vocabulary, retrieved from: https://www.electropedia.org</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b6319c74_d2ce_48c0_a75a_63156776b302</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">the anode is the negative electrode during discharge and the positive electrode during charge</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.org/dc/terms/source"/>
            <Literal>J. M. Pingarrón et al., Terminology of electrochemical methods of analysis (IUPAC Recommendations 2019), Pure and Applied Chemistry, 4, 92, 2020, 641-694. https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b6319c74_d2ce_48c0_a75a_63156776b302</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">the concepts of “anode” and “cathode” are related only to the direction of electron flow, not to the polarity of the electrodes</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b6319c74_d2ce_48c0_a75a_63156776b302</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Anode</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b66d6553_6136_4754_902a_707e414210c2</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">the lower end of the interval of frequencies tested in impedimetry and related techniques</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b66d6553_6136_4754_902a_707e414210c2</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">LowerFrequencyLimit</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_26bf1bef_d192_4da6_b0eb_d2209698fb54"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b76a778f_253f_4210_a67f_fb6444d0de26</IRI>
        <Literal>https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q962292</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.org/dc/terms/source"/>
            <Literal>J. M. Pingarrón et al., Terminology of electrochemical methods of analysis (IUPAC Recommendations 2019), Pure and Applied Chemistry, 4, 92, 2020, 641-694. https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b76a778f_253f_4210_a67f_fb6444d0de26</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">mercury electrode formed by sequence of mercury drops falling from a small aperture</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b76a778f_253f_4210_a67f_fb6444d0de26</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dropping_mercury_electrode</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b76a778f_253f_4210_a67f_fb6444d0de26</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Other kinds of mercury drop electrodes are the static mercury drop electrode (SMDE), in which a drop is held at constant radius before being mechanically knocked off, and the hanging mercury drop elec- trode (HMDE), in which the whole experiment is performed on a single drop of mercury.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b76a778f_253f_4210_a67f_fb6444d0de26</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The DME was introduced by Jaroslav Heyrovský (1890 – 1967). A drop is renewed every few seconds. Many metals amalgamate with mercury so that in the analysis of metal ions in aqueous solution the DME maintains a fresh surface throughout.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b76a778f_253f_4210_a67f_fb6444d0de26</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">DME</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b76a778f_253f_4210_a67f_fb6444d0de26</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">DroppingMercuryElectrode</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b7781ebc_90a7_4f19_997f_aed28dee1b01</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">TheoreticalChargeCapacity</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b7781ebc_90a7_4f19_997f_aed28dee1b01</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">TheoreticalCapacity</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_50c298c2_55a2_4068_b3ac_4e948c33181f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b79d4f9e_5727_4895_8d7f_5fc18d83eb90</IRI>
        <Literal>https://www.electropedia.org/iev/iev.nsf/display?openform&amp;ievref=114-02-07</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_50c298c2_55a2_4068_b3ac_4e948c33181f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b79d4f9e_5727_4895_8d7f_5fc18d83eb90</IRI>
        <Literal>https://www.electropedia.org/iev/iev.nsf/display?openform&amp;ievref=482-03-13</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.org/dc/terms/source"/>
            <Literal>International Electrotechnical Commission (IEC), IEC 60050 - International Electrotechnical Vocabulary, retrieved from: https://www.electropedia.org</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b79d4f9e_5727_4895_8d7f_5fc18d83eb90</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">an unwanted side reaction</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://purl.org/dc/terms/source"/>
            <Literal>International Electrotechnical Commission (IEC), IEC 60050 - International Electrotechnical Vocabulary, retrieved from: https://www.electropedia.org</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_b79d4f9e_5727_4895_8d7f_5fc18d83eb90</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">in a galvanic cell, parasitic reactions may cause inefficiencies or loss of performance</Literal>
