        <Literal xml:lang="en">https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_d95fdd2e_38bf_4065_a857_78eb201ee166</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A linear potential scan, at sufficiently slow scan rates so as to ensure a steady state response, is usually applied.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_d95fdd2e_38bf_4065_a857_78eb201ee166</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Mass transport of a redox species enhanced by convection in this way results in a greater electric current. Convective mass transfer occurs up to the diffusion-limiting layer, within which the mass transfer is controlled by diffusion. Electroactive substance depletion outside the diffusion layer is annulled by convective mass transfer, which results in steady- state sigmoidal wave-shaped current-potential curves.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/electrochemistry#electrochemistry_d95fdd2e_38bf_4065_a857_78eb201ee166</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The forced flow can be accomplished by movement either of the solution (solution stirring, or channel flow), or of the electrode (electrode rotation or vibration).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
