    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02122e58_e0b3_4274_bdd4_745f64a61645</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A building or group of buildings where goods are manufactured or assembled.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02122e58_e0b3_4274_bdd4_745f64a61645</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">IndustrialPlant</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_705f27ae_954c_4f13_98aa_18473fc52b25"/>
            <Literal xml:lang="en">From Latin factor, from fact- ‘done’, from the verb facere (to do).</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02122e58_e0b3_4274_bdd4_745f64a61645</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Factory</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_31252f35_c767_4b97_a877_1235076c3e13"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_0277f24a_ea7f_4917_81b7_fb0406c8fc62</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">An holistic perspective considers each part of the whole as equally important, without the need to position the parts within a hierarchy (in time or space). The interest is on the whole object and on its parts (how they contribute to the whole, i.e. their roles), without going further into specifying the spatial hierarchy or the temporal position of each part.

This class allows the picking of parts without necessarily going trough a rigid hierarchy of spatial compositions (e.g. body -&gt; organ -&gt; cell -&gt; molecule) or temporal composition. This is inline with the transitive nature of parthood, as it is usually defined in literature.

The holistic perspective is not excluding the reductionistic perspective, on the contrary it can be considered its complement.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_70fe84ff_99b6_4206_a9fc_9a8931836d84"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_0277f24a_ea7f_4917_81b7_fb0406c8fc62</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The union of classes whole and part.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_0277f24a_ea7f_4917_81b7_fb0406c8fc62</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A perspective characterized by the belief that some mereological parts of a whole (holistic parts) are intimately interconnected and explicable only by reference to the whole and vice versa.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_b432d2d5_25f4_4165_99c5_5935a7763c1a"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_0277f24a_ea7f_4917_81b7_fb0406c8fc62</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A molecule of a body can have role in the body evolution, without caring if its part of a specific organ and without specifying the time interval in which this role occurred.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_b432d2d5_25f4_4165_99c5_5935a7763c1a"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_0277f24a_ea7f_4917_81b7_fb0406c8fc62</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A product is a role that can be fulfilled by many objects, but always requires a process to which the product participates and from which it is generated.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_705f27ae_954c_4f13_98aa_18473fc52b25"/>
            <Literal xml:lang="en">From the word &apos;holistic&apos; with the &apos;w-&apos; prefix, due to the affinity with the existing word &apos;whole&apos;, that share the same meaning of &apos;holos&apos;.</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_0277f24a_ea7f_4917_81b7_fb0406c8fc62</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Wholistic</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_705f27ae_954c_4f13_98aa_18473fc52b25"/>
            <Literal xml:lang="en">Holism (from Greek ὅλος holos &quot;all, whole, entire&quot;).</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_0277f24a_ea7f_4917_81b7_fb0406c8fc62</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Holistic</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02a935c8_c2d4_4a00_bd6f_b89d05aac79e</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">PerTemperatureUnit</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02c0621e_a527_4790_8a0f_2bb51973c819</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A &apos;Mathematical&apos; entity that is made of a &apos;Numeral&apos; and a &apos;MeasurementUnit&apos; defined by a physical law, connected to a physical entity through a model perspective. Measurement is done according to the same model.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02c0621e_a527_4790_8a0f_2bb51973c819</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">In the same system of quantities, dim ρB = ML−3 is the quantity dimension of mass concentration of component B, and ML−3 is also the quantity dimension of mass density, ρ.
ISO 80000-1</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02c0621e_a527_4790_8a0f_2bb51973c819</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Measured or simulated &apos;physical propertiy&apos;-s are always defined by a physical law, connected to a physical entity through a model perspective and measurement is done according to the same model.

Systems of units suggests that this is the correct approach, since except for the fundamental units (length, time, charge) every other unit is derived by mathematical relations between these fundamental units, implying a physical laws or definitions.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02c0621e_a527_4790_8a0f_2bb51973c819</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Measurement units of quantities of the same quantity dimension may be designated by the same name and symbol even when the quantities are not of the same kind.

For example, joule per kelvin and J/K are respectively the name and symbol of both a measurement unit of heat capacity and a measurement unit of entropy, which are generally not considered to be quantities of the same kind.

However, in some cases special measurement unit names are restricted to be used with quantities of specific kind only.

For example, the measurement unit ‘second to the power minus one’ (1/s) is called hertz (Hz) when used for frequencies and becquerel (Bq) when used for activities of radionuclides.

As another example, the joule (J) is used as a unit of energy, but never as a unit of moment of force, i.e. the newton metre (N · m).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02c0621e_a527_4790_8a0f_2bb51973c819</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">— quantities of the same kind have the same quantity dimension,
— quantities of different quantity dimensions are always of different kinds, and
— quantities having the same quantity dimension are not necessarily of the same kind.
ISO 80000-1</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02c0621e_a527_4790_8a0f_2bb51973c819</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">PhysicalQuantity</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02c4890b_aef3_4173_9669_94d1f6baf611</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Coupled</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02e894c3_b793_4197_b120_3442e08f58d1</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">TimeUnit</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_02face50_43a1_40ce_a909_dfe54d5e186b</IRI>
        <Literal>The owl:dataProperty that provides a serialisation of an EMMO string data entity.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
