        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_04cf0295_3e8f_4693_a87f_3130d125cf05</IRI>
        <Literal>4-9.2</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_fe015383_afb3_44a6_ae86_043628697aa2"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_04cf0295_3e8f_4693_a87f_3130d125cf05</IRI>
        <Literal>https://doi.org/10.1351/goldbook.W06668</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_04cf0295_3e8f_4693_a87f_3130d125cf05</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Force of gravity acting on a body.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_04cf0295_3e8f_4693_a87f_3130d125cf05</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Weight</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_04f2a2d5_e799_4692_a654_420e76f5acc1</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Gas is a compressible fluid, a state of matter that has no fixed shape and no fixed volume.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_04f2a2d5_e799_4692_a654_420e76f5acc1</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Gas</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_0527413c_b286_4e9c_b2d0_03fb2a038dee</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The entity (or agent, or observer, or cognitive entity) who connects &apos;Sign&apos;, &apos;Interpretant&apos; and &apos;Object&apos;.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_b432d2d5_25f4_4165_99c5_5935a7763c1a"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_0527413c_b286_4e9c_b2d0_03fb2a038dee</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">For example, the ontologist may be interest in cataloguing in the EMMO how the same object (e.g. a cat) is addressed using different signs (e.g. cat, gatto, chat) by different interpreters (e.g. english, italian or french people).

The same applies for the results of measurements: the ontologist may be interest to represent in the EMMO how different measurement processes (i.e. semiosis) lead to different quantitative results (i.e. signs) according to different measurement devices (i.e. interpreters).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_0527413c_b286_4e9c_b2d0_03fb2a038dee</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The interpreter is not the ontologist, being the ontologist acting outside the ontology at the meta-ontology level. 

On the contrary, the interpreter is an agent recognized by the ontologist. The semiotic branch of the EMMO is the tool used by the ontologist to represent an interpreter&apos;s semiotic activity.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_0527413c_b286_4e9c_b2d0_03fb2a038dee</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Interpreter</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_052e9796_1144_43ae_a798_c5755cd6cd81</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">AmountSquareTimePerMassVolumeUnit</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_054af807_85cd_4a13_8eba_119dfdaaf38b</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The interpreter&apos;s internal representation of the object in a semiosis process.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_054af807_85cd_4a13_8eba_119dfdaaf38b</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Interpretant</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_057d0573_6ac0_4c27_9e3f_3c29205fd104</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">hasDeducer</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_057e7d57_aff0_49de_911a_8861d85cef40</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A discrete data whose elements can be decoded as tokens from one or more alphabets, without necessarily respecting syntactic rules.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_b432d2d5_25f4_4165_99c5_5935a7763c1a"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_057e7d57_aff0_49de_911a_8861d85cef40</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">fe780
emmo
!5*a
cat
for(i=0;i&lt;N;++i)</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_057e7d57_aff0_49de_911a_8861d85cef40</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A symbolic entity is not necessarily graphical (e.g. it doesn&apos;t necessarily have the physical shape of a letter), but its elements can be decoded and put in relation with an alphabet.
In other words, a sequence of bit &quot;1000010&quot; in a RAM (a non-graphical entity) is a valid symbol since it can be decoded through ASCII rules as the letter &quot;B&quot;. The same holds for an entity standing for the sound of a voice saying: &quot;Hello&quot;, since it can be decomposed in discrete parts, each of them being associated to a letter of an alphabet.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_057e7d57_aff0_49de_911a_8861d85cef40</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A symbolic object possesses a reductionistic oriented structure.
For example, text is made of words, spaces and punctuations. Words are made of characters (i.e. atomic symbols).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_705f27ae_954c_4f13_98aa_18473fc52b25"/>
            <Literal>From Ancient Greek σύμβολον (súmbolon, “a sign by which one infers something; a mark, token, badge, ticket, tally, check, a signal, watchword, outward sign”), from συμβάλλω (sumbállō, “I throw together, dash together, compare, correspond, tally, come to a conclusion”), from σύν (sún, “with, together”) + βάλλω (bállō, “I throw, put”).</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_057e7d57_aff0_49de_911a_8861d85cef40</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Symbolic</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
