    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_3397f270_dfc1_4500_8f6f_4d0d85ac5f71</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">An entity is called essential if removing one direct part will lead to a change in entity class.

An entity is called redundand if removing one direct part will not lead to a change in entity class.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_3397f270_dfc1_4500_8f6f_4d0d85ac5f71</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">This definition states that this object is a non-periodic set of atoms or a set with a finite periodicity.

Removing an atom from the state will result in another type of atom_based state.

e.g. you cannot remove H from H₂0 without changing the molecule type (essential). However, you can remove a C from a nanotube (redundant). C60 fullerene is a molecule, since it has a finite periodicity and is made of a well defined number of atoms (essential). A C nanotube is not a molecule, since it has an infinite periodicity (redundant).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_3397f270_dfc1_4500_8f6f_4d0d85ac5f71</IRI>
