        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_43e9a05d_98af_41b4_92f6_00f79a09bfce</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A process can be defined only according to an entity type. The minimum process is an entity made of two entities of the same type that are temporally related.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_43e9a05d_98af_41b4_92f6_00f79a09bfce</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Following the common definition of process, the reader may think that every whole should be a process, since every 4D object always has a time dimension. However, in the EMMO we restrict the meaning of the word process to items whose evolution in time have a particular meaning for the ontologist (i.e. every 4D object unfolds in time, but not every 4D time unfolding may be of interest for the ontologist and categorized as a process).

For this reason, the definition of every specific process subclass requires the introduction of a primitive concept.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_43e9a05d_98af_41b4_92f6_00f79a09bfce</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Occurrent</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
