    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_49267eba_5548_4163_8f36_518d65b583f9</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The class of causal objects that stand for world objects according to a specific representational perspective.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_49267eba_5548_4163_8f36_518d65b583f9</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">This class is the practical implementation of the EMMO pluralistic approach for which the only objective categorization is provided by the Universe individual and all the Quantum individuals.
Between these two extremes, there are several subjective ways to categorize real world objects, each one provide under a &apos;Perspective&apos; subclass.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_705f27ae_954c_4f13_98aa_18473fc52b25"/>
            <Literal xml:lang="en">From medieval Latin perspectiva ‘(science of) optics’, from perspect- ‘looked at closely’, from the verb perspicere, from per- ‘through’ + specere ‘to look’.</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_49267eba_5548_4163_8f36_518d65b583f9</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Perspective</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_494b372c_cfdf_47d3_a4de_5e037c540de8</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">An object which is instrumental for reaching a particular purpose through its characteristic functioning process, with particular reference to mechanical or electronic equipment.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_705f27ae_954c_4f13_98aa_18473fc52b25"/>
            <Literal xml:lang="en">From French équipement, from équiper ‘equip’.</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_494b372c_cfdf_47d3_a4de_5e037c540de8</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Equipment</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_705f27ae_954c_4f13_98aa_18473fc52b25"/>
            <Literal xml:lang="en">From Latin māchina (“a machine, engine, contrivance, device, stratagem, trick”), from Doric Greek μᾱχᾰνᾱ́ (mākhanā́), cognate with Attic Greek μηχᾰνή (mēkhanḗ, “a machine, engine, contrivance, device”), from which comes mechanical.</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_494b372c_cfdf_47d3_a4de_5e037c540de8</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Machine</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_705f27ae_954c_4f13_98aa_18473fc52b25"/>
            <Literal xml:lang="en">From Old French &quot;deviser&quot;, meaning: arrange, plan, contrive. Literally &quot;dispose in portions,&quot; from Vulgar Latin &quot;divisare&quot;, frequentative of Latin dividere, meaning &quot;to divide&quot;.</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_494b372c_cfdf_47d3_a4de_5e037c540de8</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Device</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_49804605_c0fe_4538_abda_f70ba1dc8a5d</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A process which is an holistic spatial part of a process.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_b432d2d5_25f4_4165_99c5_5935a7763c1a"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_49804605_c0fe_4538_abda_f70ba1dc8a5d</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Breathing is a subprocess of living for a human being.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_49804605_c0fe_4538_abda_f70ba1dc8a5d</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">In the EMMO the relation of participation to a process falls under mereotopology.

Since topological connection means causality, then the only way for a real world object to participate to a process is to be a part of it.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_49804605_c0fe_4538_abda_f70ba1dc8a5d</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">SubProcess</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_498aad49_f8d4_40a4_a9eb_efd563a0115f</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A suspension of liquid droplets dispersed in a gas through an atomization process.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_498aad49_f8d4_40a4_a9eb_efd563a0115f</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Spray</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_31252f35_c767_4b97_a877_1235076c3e13"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_499e24a5_5072_4c83_8625_fe3f96ae4a8d</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A temporal relation between two entities occurs when the two entities are in a one directional causality relation. The idea is that a temporal relation always implies a one-directional causality between two entities, leading to a asymmetric relation.
This means that the causing entity can be in direct and optionally indirect causality relation with the effect entity. On the contrary, the effect entity cannot be in any causal relation (direct or indirect) with the causing entity.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_499e24a5_5072_4c83_8625_fe3f96ae4a8d</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A time contact occurs when x isDirectCause y and not(y isCauseOf x).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_499e24a5_5072_4c83_8625_fe3f96ae4a8d</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Each pair of entities in direct causality relation is either in hasNext or hasTwoWayCauseWith relation. The two are mutually exclusive.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_499e24a5_5072_4c83_8625_fe3f96ae4a8d</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">This relation is asymmetric and irreflexive.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_499e24a5_5072_4c83_8625_fe3f96ae4a8d</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">isBefore</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_499e24a5_5072_4c83_8625_fe3f96ae4a8d</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">hasNext</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_4a1c73f1_b6f5_4d10_a3a6_5de90bac7cd0</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A characteriser that declares a property for an object without actually interact with it with the specific interaction required by the property definition (i.e. infer a property from other properties).</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_4a1c73f1_b6f5_4d10_a3a6_5de90bac7cd0</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Estimator</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_4a464c8d_8895_44a8_a628_aed13509f1bd</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">An heterogeneous mixture that contains coarsly dispersed particles (no Tyndall effect), that generally tend to separate in time to the dispersion medium phase.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_4a464c8d_8895_44a8_a628_aed13509f1bd</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Suspensions show no significant effect on light.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
