    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_6786b336_e982_4759_8dee_1905a4106591</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The superproperty of the relations between a whole and its mereological parts that are still holistic wholes of the same type.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_b432d2d5_25f4_4165_99c5_5935a7763c1a"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_6786b336_e982_4759_8dee_1905a4106591</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A volume of water has redundand parts other volumes of water. All this volumes have holistic parts some water molecules.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_6786b336_e982_4759_8dee_1905a4106591</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The purpose of this relation is to provide a parhood relation that does not go deep enough, in terms of decomposition, to break the holistic definition of the whole.

On the contrary, the holistic parthood, is expected to go that deep.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
