    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c84c6752_6d64_48cc_9500_e54a3c34898d"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_6795a4b8_ffd0_4588_a581_a9413fe49cac</IRI>
        <Literal>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-SI_units_mentioned_in_the_SI</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_6795a4b8_ffd0_4588_a581_a9413fe49cac</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">This is a list of units that are not defined as part of the International System of Units (SI), but are otherwise mentioned in the SI brouchure, because either the General Conference on Weights and Measures (CGPM) accepts their use as being multiples or submultiples of SI-units, they have important contemporary application worldwide, or are otherwise commonly encountered worldwide.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_6795a4b8_ffd0_4588_a581_a9413fe49cac</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">SIAcceptedUnit</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_67c70dcd_2adf_4e6c_b3f8_f33dd1512487</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A computational application that uses an empiric equation to predict the behaviour of a system without relying on the knowledge of the actual physical phenomena occurring in the object.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_67c70dcd_2adf_4e6c_b3f8_f33dd1512487</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">EmpiricalSimulationSoftware</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="rdfs:comment"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_67fc0a36_8dcb_4ffa_9a43_31074efa3296</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Relates a quantity to its metrological reference through a semiotic process.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty abbreviatedIRI="owl:versionInfo"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_67fc0a36_8dcb_4ffa_9a43_31074efa3296</IRI>
        <Literal>In EMMO version 1.0.0-beta5, physical quantities used the hasMetrologicalReference object property to relate them to their units via physical dimensionality. This was simplified in 1.0.0-alpha3 in order to make reasoning faster.

The restriction (e.g. for the physical quantity Length)

    Length hasMetrologicalReference only (hasPhysicsDimension only LengthDimension)

was in 1.0.0-alpha3 changed to

    Length hasPhysicsDimension some LengthDimension

Likewise were the universal restrictions on the corresponding unit changed to excistential. E.g.

    Metre hasPhysicsDimension only LengthDimension

was changed to

    Metre hasPhysicsDimension some LengthDimension

The label of this class was also changed from PhysicsDimension to PhysicalDimension.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_67fc0a36_8dcb_4ffa_9a43_31074efa3296</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">hasMetrologicalReference</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
