        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_bbca6dfa_7463_4e8d_8280_35862ff50ce0</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">A whole possessing some proper parts of its same type.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_b432d2d5_25f4_4165_99c5_5935a7763c1a"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_bbca6dfa_7463_4e8d_8280_35862ff50ce0</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">An object A which is classified as water-fluid possesses a proper part B which is water itself if the lenght scale of the B is larger than the water intermolecular distance keeping it in the continuum range. In this sense, A is redundant.

If A is a water-fluid so small that its every proper part is no more a continuum object (i.e. no more a fluid), then A is fundamental.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
