    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_31252f35_c767_4b97_a877_1235076c3e13"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_d67ee67e_4fac_4676_82c9_aec361dba698</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">We say that an entity causes another if there is a quantum part of the first that is in causal relation with a quantum parts of the second.
An entity cannot cause itself (causal loops are forbidden) or a part of itself. For this reasons causality between entities excludes reflexivity and prevents them to overlap.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_967080e5_2f42_4eb2_a3a9_c58143e835f9"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_d67ee67e_4fac_4676_82c9_aec361dba698</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The relation between an individuals x and y, that holds if and only if:
a)  y having a part that is causing an effect on a part of x
b) y and x non-overlapping</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
            <Literal>Due to the transitivity characteristic of :overlaps subclasses, that makes it a composite property.</Literal>
        </Annotation>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c6e77b51_681b_4d04_b20d_a08f2b977470"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_d67ee67e_4fac_4676_82c9_aec361dba698</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">:isCauseOf owl:propertyDisjointWith :overlaps</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_d67ee67e_4fac_4676_82c9_aec361dba698</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">Each pair of causally connected entities is either in isDirectCauseOf or isIndirectCauseOf relation. The two are mutually exclusive.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_d67ee67e_4fac_4676_82c9_aec361dba698</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">It applies to both quantums and macro-entities (entities made of more than one quantum). It is admissible for two entities to be one the cause of the other, excepts when they are both quantums.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_c7b62dd7_063a_4c2a_8504_42f7264ba83f"/>
        <IRI>http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_d67ee67e_4fac_4676_82c9_aec361dba698</IRI>
        <Literal xml:lang="en">The OWL 2 DL version of the EMMO introduces this object property as primitive causal relation. It refers to the macro causality relation mC(x,y), defined in the EMMO FOL version. 
While the EMMO FOL introduces the quantum causality relation C(x,y) as primitive, the OWL 2 DL version substantially simplifies the theory, neglecting these lower level relations that are well above DL expressivity.</Literal>
    </AnnotationAssertion>
    <AnnotationAssertion>
        <Annotation>
            <AnnotationProperty IRI="http://emmo.info/emmo#EMMO_705f27ae_954c_4f13_98aa_18473fc52b25"/>
            <Literal xml:lang="en">From Latin causa (“reason, sake, cause”).</Literal>
        </Annotation>
